 ## Overview
-The Ideal Reconstruction Function for 3D Splatting is a rotationally symmetric, tri-cubic kernel designed to eliminate Mach bands and visible individual splats in volume rendering by ensuring smooth overlap from any viewing direction [S1, unofficial]. Unlike Gaussian splats which have infinite support, this kernel has a well-defined finite extent, providing higher visual quality at the cost of increased performance penalties for large screen-space splats [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Polynomial and Spline Kernels](9995-polynomial-and-spline-kernels.md) by optimizing the transition between inner and outer polynomial segments to minimize reconstruction variance.
+The Ideal Reconstruction Function for 3D Splatting is a rotationally symmetric, compact-support kernel designed to reconstruct 3D volume data via splatting with minimal visible artifacts [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of "Mach bands" and visible individual splat boundaries that occur when approximating smooth volume data with polygon collections or non-optimized kernels [S1, unofficial]. Unlike Gaussian splats, this kernel has a well-defined finite overlap extent and goes to zero in a minimum extent, providing high-quality smooth overlap from any viewing direction at the cost of higher performance penalties for large screen-space splats [S1, unofficial]. It is distinct from its siblings by being mathematically optimized to minimize the relative variance of a constant cube reconstruction in 3D, rather than relying on standard spline continuity constraints alone [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:**
-*   3D voxel grid data.
-*   Camera parameters (view direction, projection matrix).
-*   Kernel parameters: support radius $t = 1.556228$ and transition radius $s = 0.889392$ [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Outputs:**
-*   2D screen-space footprint function $f(x,y)$ for each splat.
-*   Final composited image with minimal intensity variation (relative variance 0.00119) [S1, unofficial].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** A 3D voxel grid with intensity values; a viewing direction; a splat center $(0,0,0)$ in local coordinates.
+*   **Output:** A 3D reconstruction kernel function $h(x,y,z)$ and its 2D projected footprint $f(x,y)$ for rasterization.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   **Kernel Function $g(r)$:** A piecewise cubic polynomial defined by radius $r = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}$.
-*   **Footprint Function $f(x,y)$:** The integral of the 3D kernel along the view direction (z-axis in local splat space).
+The kernel is defined by two scalar parameters $s$ and $t$, and two cubic polynomial coefficient sets.
+*   $t$: Support radius (outer boundary).
+*   $s$: Transition radius (inner boundary).
+*   $g(r)$: Radial function dependent on $r = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}$.
 
