 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Mach Bands:** Using polygon approximations (15-21 triangles) instead of this smooth kernel causes visible Mach bands and individual splats [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Precision:** The maximum deviation from mean intensity is small (0.00534), fitting within 8-bit color accuracy (approx. 1 bit) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Large screen-space splats incur a higher performance penalty compared to Gaussian splats due to the finite support and complex footprint calculation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Mach Bands:** Using polygon approximations (15-21 triangles) for splats causes visible Mach bands and individual splat visibility because they do not overlap smoothly [S1, unofficial]. This kernel eliminates this by providing smooth, analytic overlap.
+*   **Precision:** The maximum relative deviation from the mean for a constant cube reconstruction is small ($0.00534$), but intensities may need scaling to maintain accuracy in 8-bit color buffers [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Viewing Direction:** The kernel is rotationally symmetric, ensuring accuracy from any viewing direction, unlike axis-aligned approximations [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Determined by the number of pixels covered by the footprint. The footprint support radius is $t=1.556228$ in voxel units. In screen space, this expands based on perspective projection.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low; only requires storing the kernel parameters and the current pixel buffer.
-*   **Performance:** Slower than Gaussian splats for large splats due to the need to evaluate the closed-form integral or precomputed lookup tables for the footprint [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Quality:** Excellent. Minimizes relative variance to 0.00119, ensuring smooth density reconstruction from any viewing angle [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When visual quality is paramount and Mach bands are unacceptable, particularly for scalar and vector field visualization [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When real-time performance on limited hardware is critical and slight visual artifacts are tolerable.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Evaluating the kernel is $O(1)$ per sample. Computing the 2D footprint requires a closed-form integral, which is also $O(1)$ but involves more complex arithmetic than simple polynomial evaluation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ as it is a parametric function.
+*   **Performance:** Higher performance penalty for large screen-space splats compared to Gaussian splats due to the finite support and complex footprint calculation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Quality:** Minimizes relative variance of constant cube reconstruction to $0.00119$ [S1, unofficial]. Maximum projected footprint deviation is approximately $1/256$ of the mean, fitting within 8-bit color accuracy [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When high-quality, artifact-free volume rendering is required and polygon-based splatting is insufficient.
+*   **When not to use:** When performance is critical and large screen-space splats are common, or when infinite support (Gaussian) is acceptable for smoother falloff.
 
 ## Background
-The method was developed by Laur and Hanrahan, building on Max's 2D ideal reconstruction function [Max92] [S1, unofficial]. It was designed to replace polygon approximations of splats on SGI VGX hardware, which suffered from Mach bands [S1, unofficial]. The optimization minimizes the relative variance of the reconstruction of a constant cube, ensuring smooth overlap from any viewing direction [S1, unofficial].
+The method was developed by Laur and Hanrahan, building on Max's 2D ideal reconstruction function [Max92] [S1, unofficial]. While Max's function was designed for 2D image reconstruction, Laur and Hanrahan extended it to 3D signals to replace polygon approximations of splats in volume rendering [S1, unofficial]. The optimization minimizes the relative variance of a constant cube reconstruction, ensuring smooth overlap from any viewing direction [S1, unofficial]. It was implemented using the fast compositing hardware of the SGI VGX [S1, unofficial].
 
